WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.760
- I think I've only had beets like twice,

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:03.040
two times in my life.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:04.023
- [Flynn] Is this the second time?

00:00:04.023 --> 00:00:05.372
- [Inga] This is like the third time I think.

00:00:05.372 --> 00:00:06.205
Yeah.

00:00:06.205 --> 00:00:08.788
(lively music)

00:00:08.967 --> 00:00:12.170
^- I just heard about this restaurant that's called gem

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:14.823
that's owned by a 19 year old chef.

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:19.151
And apparently, he charges $155 per person,

00:00:19.151 --> 00:00:21.583
for a 14 course tasting menu.

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:23.040
He's invited us to check out

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:24.950
how he prepares his favorite dish.

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:26.271
Let's see what it's all about.

00:00:26.271 --> 00:00:28.854
(lively music)

00:00:30.516 --> 00:00:31.349
- [Flynn] We do two seatings a night.

00:00:31.349 --> 00:00:32.182
- [Inga] Okay.

00:00:32.182 --> 00:00:33.850
- [Flynn] So that everyone comes at the same time.

00:00:33.850 --> 00:00:35.930
So almost like, kinda like a dinner party in a way.

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:37.913
- How did you decide on this concept?

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.490
^- Well, I started to love cooking by doing these dinners

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:44.070
^in my house, or in my parents' house.

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:47.790
I started sort of just trying out little things

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:48.923
when I was like 10.

00:00:49.224 --> 00:00:52.673
And then I started getting really into it when I was 12.

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:54.210
And that's when I started doing

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:55.380
these sort of dinner parties.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.570
- What were you serving at 12?

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:59.578
- I mean it was like a full like 10 course--

00:00:59.578 --> 00:01:00.411
- [Inga] What?

00:01:00.411 --> 00:01:01.244
- Tasting menu.

00:01:01.244 --> 00:01:02.210
- Do your parents also cook?

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:03.730
- They kinda got into it because

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:04.997
I didn't really like my parent's cooking.

00:01:04.997 --> 00:01:06.018
(laughs loudly)

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:07.033
- Okay.

00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:07.866
That's fair.

00:01:07.866 --> 00:01:08.699
Yeah.

00:01:08.699 --> 00:01:10.060
(lively music)

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:11.773
Is this your favorite dish here?

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:12.853
- Yeah.

00:01:12.853 --> 00:01:13.686
In a way.

00:01:13.686 --> 00:01:14.519
- It's hard to tell what it is.

00:01:14.519 --> 00:01:15.440
- It doesn't look like much right now.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:17.103
So this is a beet.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:22.053
It's been smoked, braised, and then dried.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:23.390
The idea is we wanted to have

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:25.710
the sort of same texture of a steak.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:27.890
We just sort of move it around in the pan for a second.

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:31.670
It just creates this sort of like really kind of hard crust.

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:32.503
- Ooh, okay.

00:01:32.503 --> 00:01:33.342
- So it's soft right now and then

00:01:33.342 --> 00:01:35.556
it'll kind of dry up when we put it in the oven.

00:01:35.556 --> 00:01:37.676
Then so what happens is the sugar's gonna be outside

00:01:37.676 --> 00:01:40.010
so it all caramelizes a lot quicker.

00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:42.020
This is just a little sauce that we make.

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:45.713
So it's all of the scraps that come out of making the beet.

00:01:45.790 --> 00:01:46.623
We smoke it.

00:01:46.623 --> 00:01:51.360
And then we cook it down to make traditional steak sauce.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:52.960
- But with beets.
- But with beets.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:53.793
Yeah.

00:01:53.793 --> 00:01:57.193
We take just some of the greens from the beet,

00:01:57.460 --> 00:01:58.660
kinda like creamed spinach.

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:01.873
- So basically everything you're doing is beet related.

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:03.043
- Yes.

00:02:03.043 --> 00:02:04.333
It's all coming from a beet.

00:02:04.333 --> 00:02:06.170
- It's like an ode to beet.

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:07.471
- The funny thing about this dish is like

00:02:07.471 --> 00:02:10.963
it came out of this fascination with not loving beets.

00:02:10.963 --> 00:02:12.083
- [Inga] Oh, okay.

00:02:12.083 --> 00:02:15.704
- And wanting to make something that I thought was

00:02:15.704 --> 00:02:17.814
really delicious and interesting.

00:02:17.814 --> 00:02:20.010
And then, it comes out just like this.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:22.010
And you wouldn't even know that it took,

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:22.863
- Yeah.

00:02:22.863 --> 00:02:24.703
- many, many days to work on.

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:26.752
- So can we eat that?

00:02:26.752 --> 00:02:27.585
(laughs softly)

00:02:27.585 --> 00:02:28.418
- Yeah.

00:02:28.418 --> 00:02:30.680
- Is there a specific way I should eat it?

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:31.513
No, right?
- No.

00:02:31.513 --> 00:02:32.850
- Just like treat it as steak.

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:35.953
(lively music)

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:37.630
This is really interesting.

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:39.901
Like I can't even taste the beet with the sauce.

00:02:39.901 --> 00:02:43.920
- Well what's weird is that it's all beets.

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:45.727
So it's funny we have somebody going like,

00:02:45.727 --> 00:02:46.560
"I don't like beets."

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.020
And I can't even taste it.
- Yeah.

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:49.540
- But it's like no this is literally

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:52.510
the most beety tasting thing someone could actually get

00:02:52.510 --> 00:02:54.873
because there's nothing else in there really.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:55.733
- Yeah.

00:02:55.733 --> 00:02:56.566
My mind is like blown.

00:02:56.566 --> 00:02:58.390
So this is one out of 14 courses.

00:02:58.390 --> 00:03:00.010
Is this kind of like what you do

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:01.820
with the whole tasting menu?

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:04.570
- This is your second to last savory course.

00:03:04.570 --> 00:03:06.750
You're not only getting little tiny tasting things.

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:07.998
You're getting a few kind of bigger,

00:03:07.998 --> 00:03:10.363
more substantial courses, as well.

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:11.283
- How much?

00:03:11.283 --> 00:03:12.850
It's like 150?

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:13.870
- It's 155.

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:15.204
- 155 per person.

00:03:15.204 --> 00:03:17.457
That's like not a small sum of money.

00:03:17.457 --> 00:03:21.003
- It's not a place that people come every single night.

00:03:21.003 --> 00:03:21.836
- [Inga] Right.

00:03:21.836 --> 00:03:24.503
- And I mean, that's the idea of it.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:27.360
If you really wanted to get the best of everything,

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:29.100
and unfortunately, we're in New York City.

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:30.060
Everything's very expensive.

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:31.090
That's sort of also why we have

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:32.090
the living room in the daytime.

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:34.510
Whereas we wanted people to still be able to experience

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:37.710
the space and be in here if they couldn't afford

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:39.291
the price tag at night.

00:03:39.291 --> 00:03:41.110
This has been the goal for so long.

00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:43.447
So that sort of reset the clock of it's like,

00:03:43.447 --> 00:03:45.070
"Okay. Now I have what I've been trying

00:03:45.070 --> 00:03:46.760
to get for nine years."

00:03:46.760 --> 00:03:50.221
Now this sort of dictates everything that comes next.

00:03:50.221 --> 00:03:52.804
(lively music)

